\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Ablation comparison across six presets and 27 cognitive domains. \texttt  {HDCOnly} (Cohen's $d = 0.61$) produces the largest overall deficit, with WorM ($\Delta = 3.09$), Language ($\Delta = 2.51$), and Inhibition ($\Delta = 2.10$) most affected. \texttt  {ReducedWM} selectively devastates WorM ($\Delta = 3.09$) while leaving other domains largely intact. Several domains (InstitutionalReasoning, CausalReasoning, Security, SustainedAttention, ToMBench) are ablation-invariant ($\eta ^2 = 0.000$), reflecting reliance on structural HDC operations unaffected by encoding noise. Data loaded from \texttt  {ablation\_domains.csv}.}}{27}{figure.caption.47}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ablation}{{9}{27}{Ablation comparison across six presets and 27 cognitive domains. \texttt {HDCOnly} (Cohen's $d = 0.61$) produces the largest overall deficit, with WorM ($\Delta = 3.09$), Language ($\Delta = 2.51$), and Inhibition ($\Delta = 2.10$) most affected. \texttt {ReducedWM} selectively devastates WorM ($\Delta = 3.09$) while leaving other domains largely intact. Several domains (InstitutionalReasoning, CausalReasoning, Security, SustainedAttention, ToMBench) are ablation-invariant ($\eta ^2 = 0.000$), reflecting reliance on structural HDC operations unaffected by encoding noise. Data loaded from \texttt {ablation\_domains.csv}}{figure.caption.47}{}}
